WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:27.590
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:27.590 --> 00:00:28.510
LOW: Good afternoon.

00:00:32.479 --> 00:00:36.710
Our speakers this afternoon, of
course, need no introduction.

00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:40.060
Let me just say a
few words anyway.

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:47.290
We live in a time of
rising expectations.

00:00:47.290 --> 00:00:50.256
And we physicists, I
think, are no exception

00:00:50.256 --> 00:00:52.130
to what is going on in
the rest of the world.

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:55.030
Our expectations
are rising also.

00:00:55.030 --> 00:00:58.390
There's talk of a crisis
in elementary particle

00:00:58.390 --> 00:01:03.700
physics, nothing to do,
technological unemployment.

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:05.500
Where are we going to go?

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:07.000
All of those questions--

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:08.570
Of course, the crisis--

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:10.030
it's not really a crisis.

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:14.620
It's a reflection of
the tremendous success

00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:20.590
that this field has had
over the last decade.

00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:23.415
46 years ago-- the
date we're celebrating,

00:01:23.415 --> 00:01:26.030
the date we're
remembering, 46 years ago--

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:30.610
it would have been
inconceivable to imagine

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:34.430
that we would have the
understanding, the knowledge

00:01:34.430 --> 00:01:38.890
of the subnuclear
world that we now have.

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:43.720
So it's hardly a
crisis in that sense.

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:45.980
It's a wonderful accomplishment.

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:51.550
It's been reflected a great deal
in the talks that we've heard.

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:55.990
Today, we have four
speakers, all of whom

00:01:55.990 --> 00:02:00.760
played major parts in
achieving this success

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:02.380
and learning the
things that we've

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:04.450
learned over the last 25 years.

00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:08.380
It's an honor to
have them with us.

00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:11.410
Looking at the
titles, it seems to me

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:15.790
the first three speakers
are addressing the present,

00:02:15.790 --> 00:02:17.440
the past, and the future.

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:21.410
And the last, Steve, is
addressing all of the above.

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:24.040
So we'll see.

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:26.950
The first speaker is
Professor Sam Ting.

00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:30.681
And his title is, "Muons,
Electrons, and Gamma Rays."

00:02:30.681 --> 00:02:31.180
Sam.

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:52.390
TING: It's 25 years
ago, Viki hired me.

00:02:55.930 --> 00:02:58.440
At that time, I had
quite a few offers.

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:03.760
MIT was the only
offer without tenure.

00:03:08.740 --> 00:03:21.880
But nevertheless, I sensed their
great freedom for research,

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:25.000
and, therefore, I
decided to come here.

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:26.890
In any case, I did
not really understand

00:03:26.890 --> 00:03:30.520
what tenure was in the case.

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:33.010
What I would like to
do today, in order not

00:03:33.010 --> 00:03:40.345
to waste your time, is
to describe a little bit

00:03:40.345 --> 00:03:45.670
the sense Professor Becker,
Professor Min Chen, and others

00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:48.430
have been doing, together
with me, on physics

00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:50.400
of the electrons,
muons, and photons.

00:03:54.730 --> 00:04:00.330
The first period
covers from '65 to '72,

00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:03.910
in the Deutches Elektonen
Synchrotron in Hamburg.

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:07.240
My personal interest
in this field

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:15.690
comes from reading a paper from
Columbia, by Francis Low, whom

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:20.670
I did not know at that time, on
the possibility of an excited

00:04:20.670 --> 00:04:27.320
electron [? go to econ. ?]
And that paper

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:30.730
fascinated me a great deal.

00:04:30.730 --> 00:04:35.670
As you all know, the physics of
the electron positron, in fact,

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:40.090
[INAUDIBLE] this morning,
already since '48, '49,

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:43.000
and '50s, through the work
of Deutsch and Kendall

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:46.180
and others, who have the
simplest electron positron

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:47.110
collider.

00:04:47.110 --> 00:04:50.530
And that is positronium.

00:04:50.530 --> 00:04:55.720
In 1964, there was an
experiment that shows

00:04:55.720 --> 00:04:59.100
electrodynamics was violated.

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:02.600
The experiment that
was carried out

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:06.500
was a photoproduction of
the electron positron pair

00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:08.110
in the field of
Coloumb nucleus, where

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:11.290
you have a momentum transfer,
so-called Bethe-Heitler pair,

00:05:11.290 --> 00:05:13.540
a momentum transfer of 1 TeV.

00:05:13.540 --> 00:05:18.280
And therefore, you probe the
electron propagator to 10

00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:22.300
to the minus 14 centimeter.

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:27.460
That experiment
shows a violation

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:30.110
of the electrodynamics, as
a function of the e plus,

00:05:30.110 --> 00:05:31.780
e minus environments.

00:05:34.670 --> 00:05:39.050
Together with
Professor Becker, we

00:05:39.050 --> 00:05:47.070
built a spectrometer, which
has a magnet and detectors.

00:05:47.070 --> 00:05:50.770
The detectors defined the
aperture not the magnet.

00:05:50.770 --> 00:05:54.354
And also, two sets of Cherenkov
counters, separated by magnets,

00:05:54.354 --> 00:05:56.770
so they're not counting the
electrons from the first one--

00:05:56.770 --> 00:05:58.510
does not enter the second one.

00:05:58.510 --> 00:06:01.720
And also, the electron
are measured twice, once

00:06:01.720 --> 00:06:07.780
with the shower and the
other time with the Cherenkov

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:09.240
counter.

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:13.440
This is a picture of this
first spectrometer we made.

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:16.374
And this person
is Stewart Smith,

00:06:16.374 --> 00:06:18.790
who is now the head of the
physics department at Princeton

00:06:18.790 --> 00:06:19.814
University.

00:06:24.170 --> 00:06:29.430
The measurement from us shows
we had a small disagreement

00:06:29.430 --> 00:06:31.446
with earlier measurement.

00:06:31.446 --> 00:06:33.320
In other words, we agreed
with the prediction

00:06:33.320 --> 00:06:35.715
of the electrodynamics,
[? except ?]

00:06:35.715 --> 00:06:38.252
for the [? mass of ?] rho,
the [? mass ?] [? of ?] phi,

00:06:38.252 --> 00:06:39.210
and with the deviation.

00:06:39.210 --> 00:06:40.980
This deviation
come from the fact

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:43.770
the photon changes after
[? rho, ?] goes back

00:06:43.770 --> 00:06:47.970
to a photon, goes to the
electron positron pair.

00:06:47.970 --> 00:06:52.350
Indeed, at that time,
there were a family

00:06:52.350 --> 00:06:53.460
of three vector mesons.

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:56.080
They have the same
quantum number as a photon

00:06:56.080 --> 00:06:58.770
except it has a
mass not equal to 0,

00:06:58.770 --> 00:07:00.360
which I will call heavy photons.

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:12.580
At that time, most of the
people would discuss the vector

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:13.440
dominance model.

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:15.860
And I see Bernie
Margolis is here.

00:07:15.860 --> 00:07:17.840
He did a lot of work on this.

00:07:17.840 --> 00:07:21.780
And one can then relate that
current with the quantities

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:25.030
from contribution from
rho, from omega, from phi.

00:07:25.030 --> 00:07:30.400
In this spirit, photoproduction
of vector mesons

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:32.270
will be deflective in nature.

00:07:32.270 --> 00:07:36.030
And this is an article or data
taken from Scientific American.

00:07:36.030 --> 00:07:39.860
It shows photoproduction of
pi plus [? phi ?] minus mass

00:07:39.860 --> 00:07:41.430
as a function of angle.

00:07:41.430 --> 00:07:44.740
The yield peaks at
the rho and peaks

00:07:44.740 --> 00:07:49.050
more forward at heavy nuclei,
like the classical diffraction

00:07:49.050 --> 00:07:50.980
scattering.

00:07:50.980 --> 00:07:56.255
Analysis of this can be carried
out in the following way.

00:07:56.255 --> 00:07:58.750
You have a photon with
a coupling constant,

00:07:58.750 --> 00:08:02.950
let's say gamma v squared or
4 pi, and go into the nucleus,

00:08:02.950 --> 00:08:05.960
and go back to pi
pair or k pairs.

00:08:05.960 --> 00:08:08.170
And so this is red,
therefore, it's

00:08:08.170 --> 00:08:11.470
a quantity of the vector
meson nuclear cross-section,

00:08:11.470 --> 00:08:13.450
nuclear density, and a coupling.

00:08:13.450 --> 00:08:16.140
If you took the ratio,
the coupling disappears.

00:08:16.140 --> 00:08:19.450
It's a measurement of the
radius and vector meson

00:08:19.450 --> 00:08:21.310
nuclear cross-section.

00:08:21.310 --> 00:08:24.590
The first thing we measured was
the Woods' action potential.

00:08:24.590 --> 00:08:26.710
And I see Dave Saxon is here.

00:08:26.710 --> 00:08:28.690
And this is a very
accurate measurement

00:08:28.690 --> 00:08:31.540
of the Saxon-Wood potential
across all the nuclei.

00:08:31.540 --> 00:08:35.600
And the accuracy is 1.12
plus/minus 0.02 to A

00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:36.100
to the 1/3.

00:08:40.780 --> 00:08:44.860
Once you have measured
that, you can also look--

00:08:44.860 --> 00:08:48.440
a vector meson decays to a
photon, and decays to e plus, e

00:08:48.440 --> 00:08:49.290
minus.

00:08:49.290 --> 00:08:52.270
In which case, you have
two couplings, one,

00:08:52.270 --> 00:08:55.756
photon goes back to meson,
with a mass of the photon,

00:08:55.756 --> 00:08:58.150
and here, with a
mass of the rho.

00:08:58.150 --> 00:09:00.630
And the question, therefore,
are the two coupling constants

00:09:00.630 --> 00:09:01.129
the same?

00:09:07.290 --> 00:09:10.980
This is the
measurement of a photon

00:09:10.980 --> 00:09:14.720
go to e plus, e minus, photon
go to rho, goes to a photon,

00:09:14.720 --> 00:09:16.170
goes to e plus, e minus.

00:09:16.170 --> 00:09:18.360
Clearly, because the
final states are the same,

00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:19.566
they have the interference.

00:09:19.566 --> 00:09:20.940
Indeed, you see
the interference.

00:09:20.940 --> 00:09:24.240
Interference is a measurement
of this coupling constant, also

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:25.260
the production phase.

00:09:28.529 --> 00:09:31.630
The results of this
series of experiments

00:09:31.630 --> 00:09:36.850
shows the coupling constant and
the mass of the vector meson,

00:09:36.850 --> 00:09:39.940
from rho to e plus,
e minus, is 0.5.

00:09:39.940 --> 00:09:46.460
And from diffraction scattering,
where the photon mass is 0,

00:09:46.460 --> 00:09:48.100
it's also 0.5.

00:09:48.100 --> 00:09:50.440
And furthermore, the rho,
nuclear, and cross-sections,

00:09:50.440 --> 00:09:52.710
[? lepton ?] and nuclear
cross-section-- at that time,

00:09:52.710 --> 00:09:55.450
there was a theory, by
[? Dahl ?] and Weisskopf--

00:09:55.450 --> 00:09:59.040
I hope Viki still remember--
and shows they are the same.

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:01.000
And more important,
the coupling constant

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:02.970
is independent of
the photon mass.

00:10:07.950 --> 00:10:12.510
At that time,
there was a puzzle.

00:10:12.510 --> 00:10:16.440
And that is, there were
experiments at CEA and Cornell

00:10:16.440 --> 00:10:19.830
to look for a rho to
the electron, omega

00:10:19.830 --> 00:10:21.820
to the electron, a
diffraction pattern.

00:10:21.820 --> 00:10:25.510
And this pattern was looked for
a long time but never observed.

00:10:25.510 --> 00:10:27.900
It is difficult, because
the branching region

00:10:27.900 --> 00:10:30.430
was very small, and, therefore,
you need a high rejection.

00:10:30.430 --> 00:10:33.690
And furthermore, the width
of omega is only 10 MeV.

00:10:33.690 --> 00:10:36.170
Therefore, you need a
very good spectrometer.

00:10:36.170 --> 00:10:38.220
And a new spectrometer
was built.

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:48.840
And from this, one
indeed observes, e plus,

00:10:48.840 --> 00:10:53.520
e minus mass spectrum, it's
not only rho, but a sharp peak

00:10:53.520 --> 00:10:55.450
from rho-omega interference.

00:10:59.770 --> 00:11:05.710
You can look the vector meson
photon analogy once more.

00:11:05.710 --> 00:11:08.080
And notice, rho goes to 2 pi.

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:11.260
Omega goes to 3 pi, therefore,
they do not interfere.

00:11:11.260 --> 00:11:13.320
But omega can go to a photon.

00:11:13.320 --> 00:11:14.770
Photon can go to a rho.

00:11:14.770 --> 00:11:16.000
Rho go to 2 pi.

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:18.880
And therefore, there must
be an omega go to 2 pi.

00:11:18.880 --> 00:11:20.620
And this, indeed,
that was looked.

00:11:20.620 --> 00:11:23.770
And I think, at that time, the
most famous upper limit was set

00:11:23.770 --> 00:11:26.600
by Jack Steinberger
and Günther Lütjens.

00:11:26.600 --> 00:11:28.890
And we have looked.

00:11:28.890 --> 00:11:30.520
Indeed, with a
good spectrometer,

00:11:30.520 --> 00:11:32.490
if we do it very
precisely, you see,

00:11:32.490 --> 00:11:36.330
on hydrogen and carbon
and lead, the red spectrum

00:11:36.330 --> 00:11:38.170
is where the rho are long.

00:11:38.170 --> 00:11:42.000
The points and the blue lines
are rho-omega interference

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:42.741
due to pi.

00:11:46.669 --> 00:11:50.460
A difficult measurement
was carried out on phi

00:11:50.460 --> 00:11:53.010
to the electron pair, because
the rate was very low.

00:11:53.010 --> 00:11:55.550
And it obtained a
phi coupling constant

00:11:55.550 --> 00:11:58.050
and also phi nuclear
and cross-section agrees

00:11:58.050 --> 00:11:58.925
with the quark model.

00:12:01.460 --> 00:12:05.960
This series of experiments
can be summarized

00:12:05.960 --> 00:12:07.297
in the following way.

00:12:07.297 --> 00:12:08.880
At that time, the
so-called Weinberg's

00:12:08.880 --> 00:12:11.750
"First Sum Rule," which is
the partial width of rho,

00:12:11.750 --> 00:12:14.600
partial width of omega,
partial width of phi

00:12:14.600 --> 00:12:15.980
must have a triangular relation.

00:12:15.980 --> 00:12:19.310
And this is the phi
measurement, omega measurement,

00:12:19.310 --> 00:12:21.280
and the red line's
the rho measurement.

00:12:21.280 --> 00:12:25.410
Indeed, the measurement agrees
with the Weinberg sum rule.

00:12:32.270 --> 00:12:34.250
So to summarize,
the early data work

00:12:34.250 --> 00:12:37.470
shows the decay of vector
mesons comes SU3 theory.

00:12:37.470 --> 00:12:41.090
The electron radius is less than
10 to the minus 14 centimeter.

00:12:41.090 --> 00:12:43.230
And the question is, are
there any more vector

00:12:43.230 --> 00:12:47.760
mesons at a higher mass?

00:12:47.760 --> 00:12:51.830
I should mention,
at that time, there

00:12:51.830 --> 00:12:54.260
are many young physicists
working with us,

00:12:54.260 --> 00:12:56.480
Joseph Asbury, who is now
the associate director

00:12:56.480 --> 00:12:58.850
of the Argonne, Professor
Min Chen, Professor Becker,

00:12:58.850 --> 00:13:02.390
Wit Busza, Professor Smith
at Princeton, and so forth.

00:13:02.390 --> 00:13:05.540
And there's Sadrozinski,
Garry Sanders, and Sau-Lan Wu,

00:13:05.540 --> 00:13:09.200
and also Robin Marshall, who
just received a distinguished

00:13:09.200 --> 00:13:10.680
chair in Manchester.

00:13:13.620 --> 00:13:22.410
It's also interesting to report
that the early measurement, was

00:13:22.410 --> 00:13:24.700
confirmed by e plus
and minus collider.

00:13:24.700 --> 00:13:26.730
And for reasons I
never understood,

00:13:26.730 --> 00:13:28.640
the collider result
was not better

00:13:28.640 --> 00:13:35.080
than the earlier measurement
by Becker and Chen and others.

00:13:35.080 --> 00:13:39.350
Now we know photon and heavy
photon are almost the same.

00:13:39.350 --> 00:13:41.410
And they do transform
into each other.

00:13:41.410 --> 00:13:45.220
And we also learned how to
use high intensity flux, of 10

00:13:45.220 --> 00:13:47.890
to the 11 gamma ray per
second, to obtain pi pair

00:13:47.890 --> 00:13:50.090
rejection larger
than 10 to the a,

00:13:50.090 --> 00:13:53.600
and to have a mass
resolution of 5 MeV.

00:13:53.600 --> 00:13:55.730
And then the question
is, as I just said,

00:13:55.730 --> 00:13:59.540
why should be only three heavy
photon that all has a mass 1

00:13:59.540 --> 00:14:00.320
GeV?

00:14:00.320 --> 00:14:04.340
And to go to higher mass,
we went to Brookhaven.

00:14:04.340 --> 00:14:08.810
So let me now
summarize the only two

00:14:08.810 --> 00:14:13.860
years I ever worked
in the United States,

00:14:13.860 --> 00:14:16.403
namely, at Brookhaven.

00:14:19.301 --> 00:14:22.430
At Brookhaven, life
is somewhat difficult.

00:14:22.430 --> 00:14:24.890
Because from a proton
on a beryllium,

00:14:24.890 --> 00:14:27.350
you produce many,
many particles.

00:14:27.350 --> 00:14:30.170
The most difficult
are the electron pair,

00:14:30.170 --> 00:14:32.750
because the radius is
1 part in 10 to the a.

00:14:32.750 --> 00:14:35.520
That means to obtain
enough electron pair,

00:14:35.520 --> 00:14:38.180
you need 10 to the 12 protons.

00:14:38.180 --> 00:14:40.190
To have a pi pair
rejection of 1%,

00:14:40.190 --> 00:14:42.550
you need a rejection of
1 part in 10 to the 10th.

00:14:45.889 --> 00:14:48.590
This is the design
of the spectrometer.

00:14:48.590 --> 00:14:50.540
Looks all the same,
but, actually, it's

00:14:50.540 --> 00:14:52.147
a very difficult spectrometer.

00:14:52.147 --> 00:14:53.355
This is looking from the top.

00:14:53.355 --> 00:14:56.050
You have three magnets,
two Cherenkov counter

00:14:56.050 --> 00:14:57.830
measures the electrons.

00:14:57.830 --> 00:15:02.260
And these detectors measure
the coordinates and measures

00:15:02.260 --> 00:15:03.100
shower.

00:15:03.100 --> 00:15:06.020
Bending is in the
vertical plane.

00:15:06.020 --> 00:15:08.930
The horizontal coordinate
measures theta.

00:15:08.930 --> 00:15:10.850
Bending is a vertical plane.

00:15:10.850 --> 00:15:14.860
And therefore, the
bending, theta and phi

00:15:14.860 --> 00:15:18.938
are decoupled from each other.

00:15:18.938 --> 00:15:22.040
So this is the spectrometer.

00:15:22.040 --> 00:15:24.780
Shows the magnet,
Cherenkov counters,

00:15:24.780 --> 00:15:27.227
and position detectors.

00:15:30.030 --> 00:15:35.530
The target was arranged
in a helium bag.

00:15:35.530 --> 00:15:41.470
And they are separated by 7.5
centimeter with 1.8 millimeter

00:15:41.470 --> 00:15:42.280
beryllium.

00:15:42.280 --> 00:15:44.980
And it's done in such a
way so that the signal

00:15:44.980 --> 00:15:47.200
can be traced from one point.

00:15:47.200 --> 00:15:49.390
The background, from
two different points,

00:15:49.390 --> 00:15:50.140
can be rejected.

00:15:54.050 --> 00:15:57.690
The magnet was measured with
a three-dimensional Hall

00:15:57.690 --> 00:15:59.456
probe, 100,000 points.

00:15:59.456 --> 00:16:01.080
In those days, there
were no computers.

00:16:01.080 --> 00:16:03.120
And it was mostly done by hand.

00:16:09.580 --> 00:16:12.340
The detector is smaller than
the magnet aperture, therefore,

00:16:12.340 --> 00:16:13.950
the detector defined acceptance.

00:16:13.950 --> 00:16:17.680
It's something we
learned from [? Daisy. ?]

00:16:17.680 --> 00:16:21.700
And the magnet bends the
charged particle to an angle,

00:16:21.700 --> 00:16:24.482
so that the detector is
never exposed to the target,

00:16:24.482 --> 00:16:26.440
never interfered with
the neutrons and photons.

00:16:30.250 --> 00:16:32.980
To have a rejection of
1 part in 10 to the 10,

00:16:32.980 --> 00:16:38.290
indeed, is quite difficult. When
you have a Cherenkov counter,

00:16:38.290 --> 00:16:40.480
if a hadron go into
a Cherenkov counter,

00:16:40.480 --> 00:16:42.530
it knocks the electron forward.

00:16:42.530 --> 00:16:44.410
And, therefore, the
pion will disappear.

00:16:44.410 --> 00:16:46.000
The electron goes forward.

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:48.910
To reduce knock-on,
we use hydrogen gas,

00:16:48.910 --> 00:16:50.950
which has a smaller
number of electrons.

00:16:50.950 --> 00:16:53.680
And we put two magnets,
so that the low energy

00:16:53.680 --> 00:16:58.145
electron does not enter the
second Cherenkov counter.

00:16:58.145 --> 00:17:00.820
And these are very nicely
made Cherenkov counters,

00:17:00.820 --> 00:17:03.700
with hydrogen, the very same
on both sides, including

00:17:03.700 --> 00:17:07.849
a mirror, which is made at CERN
workshop, 3 millimeters thick.

00:17:07.849 --> 00:17:10.830
And you see one, two,
three photoelectrons.

00:17:13.599 --> 00:17:17.500
Position-sensitive counter
were made by Ulrich Becker.

00:17:20.819 --> 00:17:22.290
There are 10,000 wires.

00:17:22.290 --> 00:17:24.960
None of them broke
during the experiment.

00:17:24.960 --> 00:17:28.440
Resolution is 1 millimeter.

00:17:28.440 --> 00:17:30.790
The three wires are
60 degrees apart,

00:17:30.790 --> 00:17:33.640
and, therefore, the sum
is always a constant,

00:17:33.640 --> 00:17:36.430
and the rate is 20
megahertz, equivalent to 10

00:17:36.430 --> 00:17:38.162
to the 36 luminosity.

00:17:38.162 --> 00:17:40.120
And they are designed
such that output's always

00:17:40.120 --> 00:17:43.332
a very low voltage
because of high radiation.

00:17:46.950 --> 00:17:49.800
To have an electron, you must
have an electron detector,

00:17:49.800 --> 00:17:51.920
and you must have a calibration.

00:17:51.920 --> 00:17:55.440
To calibrate this, we
generate, artificially,

00:17:55.440 --> 00:17:59.230
from proton on a target at pi
0, get a photon e plus, e minus.

00:17:59.230 --> 00:18:02.280
[? Then ?] [? send ?] e plus
into a Cherenkov counter,

00:18:02.280 --> 00:18:04.680
let the e minus go into
the main spectrometer.

00:18:04.680 --> 00:18:07.890
A coincidence enables you
to calibrate your detector

00:18:07.890 --> 00:18:10.850
on the electron,
artificial electron beam.

00:18:15.950 --> 00:18:18.260
Because you need 10
to the 12th protons,

00:18:18.260 --> 00:18:20.450
so you have a 10%
target, therefore,

00:18:20.450 --> 00:18:24.200
10 to the 12th
particles are produced.

00:18:24.200 --> 00:18:28.450
To stop photons,
electrons and muons,

00:18:28.450 --> 00:18:32.370
we use 5 tons of uranium,
100 tons of lead.

00:18:32.370 --> 00:18:35.600
And to stop the pion,
kaon, and so forth, we

00:18:35.600 --> 00:18:40.450
used 10,000 tons of concrete
so generously lent to us

00:18:40.450 --> 00:18:43.600
by Karl Strauch, from CEA.

00:18:43.600 --> 00:18:46.140
And soft neutrons are
still left all over.

00:18:46.140 --> 00:18:49.690
And we manage to convince Martin
Deutsch, who was the laboratory

00:18:49.690 --> 00:18:53.170
nuclear science director,
to buy five tons of soap

00:18:53.170 --> 00:18:54.610
to stop soft neutrons.

00:18:58.590 --> 00:19:06.190
And this shows, if you can see
it, the location of the soap.

00:19:06.190 --> 00:19:08.770
In fact, it's during
this experiment,

00:19:08.770 --> 00:19:12.210
we began to know Martin Deutsch.

00:19:12.210 --> 00:19:15.210
And he really supported us
and made an enormous effort

00:19:15.210 --> 00:19:16.845
to make this
experiment possible.

00:19:20.600 --> 00:19:23.600
It takes a whole week
to set up the detector,

00:19:23.600 --> 00:19:25.310
because all of the
Cherenkov counters

00:19:25.310 --> 00:19:28.330
must be mapped to make sure
they are 100% efficient.

00:19:28.330 --> 00:19:32.540
All the chamber area are mapped
to make sure they're 100%.

00:19:32.540 --> 00:19:35.100
All counter efficiency
and timing are checked.

00:19:35.100 --> 00:19:37.350
And [? both ?] side
on all shower counters

00:19:37.350 --> 00:19:40.310
are calibrated with
artificial electron beam.

00:19:40.310 --> 00:19:42.590
All voltage are checked
every 30 minutes.

00:19:42.590 --> 00:19:45.950
All magnets are
reversed once a day,

00:19:45.950 --> 00:19:48.770
mainly because you want the
rejection of 1 part in 10

00:19:48.770 --> 00:19:52.790
to the 10th, a mass
resolution of 1 part in 1,000.

00:19:52.790 --> 00:19:59.442
It was also during this time, we
began to know Herman Feshbach.

00:19:59.442 --> 00:20:03.410
And, since that was the first
experiment we are inside US,

00:20:03.410 --> 00:20:07.840
we had then close interaction
with the Laboratory Nuclear

00:20:07.840 --> 00:20:11.016
Science and, particularly,
Dr. Fred Eppling.

00:20:11.016 --> 00:20:17.250
And I have benefited enormously
from these three gentlemen.

00:20:17.250 --> 00:20:19.740
So this is the result
of this experiment.

00:20:19.740 --> 00:20:24.150
It shows a proton
on a beryllium,

00:20:24.150 --> 00:20:27.380
as a function of e plus,
[? give us ?] [INAUDIBLE] mass.

00:20:27.380 --> 00:20:30.336
Indeed, show a sharp peak,
which we call the J particle.

00:20:33.252 --> 00:20:36.920
The physicists in
this experiment,

00:20:36.920 --> 00:20:40.490
there's Becker,
Burger, Wit Burza

00:20:40.490 --> 00:20:43.360
helped to make sure the
proposal get approved,

00:20:43.360 --> 00:20:46.560
Min Chen, Sau-Lan Wu, and then
Jean-Jacques Aubert, who is now

00:20:46.560 --> 00:20:48.470
a professor in
Marseilles, Walter Toki,

00:20:48.470 --> 00:20:53.485
a student of Becker's, now
a professor at Colorado.

00:20:53.485 --> 00:20:54.870
And there's a nice picture.

00:20:58.675 --> 00:20:59.760
This is Sau-Lan Wu.

00:20:59.760 --> 00:21:00.890
This is my wife, Susan.

00:21:00.890 --> 00:21:06.050
And there's Jean-Jacques
Aubert and Walter Toki.

00:21:06.050 --> 00:21:08.940
Very young at that time.

00:21:08.940 --> 00:21:11.880
After that, to look for
higher mass particle,

00:21:11.880 --> 00:21:13.980
we did an experiment
at CERN ISR.

00:21:13.980 --> 00:21:17.399
And essentially, it was a
proton-proton collision.

00:21:17.399 --> 00:21:19.440
It goes to a photon, goes
to a mu plus, mu minus.

00:21:19.440 --> 00:21:22.740
Going to look for a new
quark and look for Q Q bar

00:21:22.740 --> 00:21:25.368
distributions.

00:21:25.368 --> 00:21:26.605
ISR is a new concept.

00:21:30.250 --> 00:21:34.060
Rings of protons and protons
collide, and, therefore,

00:21:34.060 --> 00:21:36.530
the center mass is
equal to the laboratory.

00:21:36.530 --> 00:21:39.100
It's a new type of
accelerator made

00:21:39.100 --> 00:21:45.380
possible through the
personal, strong push

00:21:45.380 --> 00:21:47.560
by Victor Weisskopf, when
he was director general.

00:21:51.500 --> 00:21:54.770
This is the ISR
experiment, where you

00:21:54.770 --> 00:21:56.610
have a proton-proton collision.

00:21:56.610 --> 00:21:59.840
These are magnetized ions from
the [INAUDIBLE] cyclotron.

00:21:59.840 --> 00:22:03.030
And these are very large
propulsion chambers

00:22:03.030 --> 00:22:06.205
developed by Ulrich Becker.

00:22:06.205 --> 00:22:10.820
And this the first time we
will enter into very large,

00:22:10.820 --> 00:22:12.510
solid [? angle ?] devices.

00:22:12.510 --> 00:22:13.690
I guess you can't see it.

00:22:13.690 --> 00:22:16.410
But we have to really take
the accelerator apart in order

00:22:16.410 --> 00:22:18.390
to install this detector.

00:22:18.390 --> 00:22:21.900
And also, ever
since that time, we

00:22:21.900 --> 00:22:23.910
have had a very
pleasant collaboration

00:22:23.910 --> 00:22:25.680
with Karl Strauch and his group.

00:22:29.420 --> 00:22:33.110
And there's the installation
of the experiment.

00:22:33.110 --> 00:22:34.640
And it shows Ulrich Becker--

00:22:34.640 --> 00:22:36.450
How much younger then.

00:22:39.020 --> 00:22:42.390
And also, this is a
picture of Professor Min

00:22:42.390 --> 00:22:46.780
Chen under the detector.

00:22:46.780 --> 00:22:53.310
And this is the large chamber
developed by Ulrich Becker.

00:22:53.310 --> 00:22:57.360
And it really made the
subsequent experiment possible.

00:22:57.360 --> 00:23:03.790
The process of collisions is
known as Drell-Yan process.

00:23:03.790 --> 00:23:05.910
So the first result
is to provide

00:23:05.910 --> 00:23:08.790
a very accurate check
of PP interaction

00:23:08.790 --> 00:23:09.840
in a time-like region.

00:23:13.640 --> 00:23:18.870
And this is a direct
comparison of scaling phenomena

00:23:18.870 --> 00:23:22.010
at s equal to 62, s
equal to 44, and shows,

00:23:22.010 --> 00:23:27.020
indeed, the scaling
phenomena is correct.

00:23:27.020 --> 00:23:31.000
But also, the second thing is
to see the effect of gluons.

00:23:33.800 --> 00:23:37.370
The first effect one can
see is the following, where

00:23:37.370 --> 00:23:40.190
you have a PP, one goes to
a photon, go to mu plus,

00:23:40.190 --> 00:23:41.192
mu minus.

00:23:41.192 --> 00:23:42.275
The other goes to a gluon.

00:23:47.060 --> 00:23:49.670
If there's a gluon emission,
as a function of energy,

00:23:49.670 --> 00:23:53.060
the transfers of momentum,
or PT, will be increased.

00:23:53.060 --> 00:23:55.470
Indeed, this is the
PT measured from us,

00:23:55.470 --> 00:23:58.400
and this is a measurement by
Lederman group at Fermilab,

00:23:58.400 --> 00:24:01.538
and shows it fits to
the QCD prediction.

00:24:04.350 --> 00:24:12.120
The second QCD effect also
concerning multiplicity.

00:24:12.120 --> 00:24:16.116
If you look at gluon emission,
which is opposite to the mu

00:24:16.116 --> 00:24:18.240
pairs, [? a local ?]
[? transfers ?] [? movement ?]

00:24:18.240 --> 00:24:21.390
of mu pairs in the
opposite direction,

00:24:21.390 --> 00:24:23.400
from where the gluon
energy increases,

00:24:23.400 --> 00:24:26.580
because of balance of momentum,
and the multiplicity increases.

00:24:30.730 --> 00:24:32.840
There were many young
physicists working

00:24:32.840 --> 00:24:35.420
at ICR, Jim Branson, who is
now a professor at University

00:24:35.420 --> 00:24:37.810
of California,
Francois Vannucci,

00:24:37.810 --> 00:24:42.350
in Paris V, [? Soji ?]
Sugimoto in Kyoto,

00:24:42.350 --> 00:24:46.100
Roberta Battiston, in Perugia,
and Pierre Spillantini now

00:24:46.100 --> 00:24:49.470
is a professor in Florence.

00:24:49.470 --> 00:24:53.880
After that, we
faced the question,

00:24:53.880 --> 00:24:56.610
where shall we go for
the next experiment,

00:24:56.610 --> 00:24:58.098
going to PETRA or PEP?

00:25:01.374 --> 00:25:05.330
I want to show this
check, which I received

00:25:05.330 --> 00:25:15.910
from Victor Weisskopf, one
of our [? author. ?] It says,

00:25:15.910 --> 00:25:19.650
lost bet as to who is
first, PETRA or PEP.

00:25:24.070 --> 00:25:25.465
Viki, I still have this check.

00:25:28.510 --> 00:25:30.170
WEISSKOPF: You didn't cash it?

00:25:30.170 --> 00:25:30.930
TING: No.

00:25:30.930 --> 00:25:32.660
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:25:32.660 --> 00:25:33.160
TING: No.

00:25:35.920 --> 00:25:39.730
At PETRA, you have a 24 GeV
e plus or 24 GeV e minus.

00:25:39.730 --> 00:25:44.210
It Goes to a photon, then goes
to quarks, and the electron.

00:25:44.210 --> 00:25:53.200
PETRA is accelerator which has
four places, TASSO, Mark-J,

00:25:53.200 --> 00:25:57.990
Jade, and Pluto and
Cella, four detectors.

00:25:57.990 --> 00:26:00.780
The Mark-J detector is
a very simple detector.

00:26:00.780 --> 00:26:03.400
Basically, it's a colorimeter,
a [? four-by ?] colorimeter.

00:26:03.400 --> 00:26:06.540
It measures hadrons, measures
muons, measures the electrons

00:26:06.540 --> 00:26:07.050
and photons.

00:26:10.335 --> 00:26:16.390
So this is the Mark-J detector
and shows the large chambers

00:26:16.390 --> 00:26:19.512
made by Becker
and collaborators.

00:26:25.190 --> 00:26:30.750
Image resolution is about
25% and covers about 95%

00:26:30.750 --> 00:26:33.730
of the total energy.

00:26:33.730 --> 00:26:41.590
During Mark-J, we had the first
group of Chinese ever sent

00:26:41.590 --> 00:26:42.120
to the West.

00:26:42.120 --> 00:26:44.620
And I went to see
Mr. Deng Xiaoping.

00:26:44.620 --> 00:26:49.350
He said, how about training
some Chinese physicists for us?

00:26:49.350 --> 00:26:50.490
And I asked, how many?

00:26:50.490 --> 00:26:52.480
He said how about 100?

00:26:52.480 --> 00:26:56.810
And I had to explain to him,
no, physicists are not soldiers.

00:26:56.810 --> 00:26:58.570
And you have to select them.

00:26:58.570 --> 00:27:00.430
And so he selected 10.

00:27:00.430 --> 00:27:04.240
And now, the new director of
the Institute of High Energy

00:27:04.240 --> 00:27:08.750
Physics and also the one in
charge of the Beijing detector,

00:27:08.750 --> 00:27:11.680
in fact, most of the active
high energy physicists

00:27:11.680 --> 00:27:13.950
come from this team.

00:27:13.950 --> 00:27:19.930
Also, in 1981, Spain decided
to rejoin High Energy Physics

00:27:19.930 --> 00:27:23.440
group and selected,
in a national way,

00:27:23.440 --> 00:27:25.480
a group of physicists to
start working with us.

00:27:25.480 --> 00:27:28.510
Juan Antonino Rubio
now is in charge

00:27:28.510 --> 00:27:30.070
of the tau/charm factory.

00:27:30.070 --> 00:27:32.800
Bernardo Adeva is a
professor in San Diego.

00:27:32.800 --> 00:27:36.980
Martinez is a
professor in Barcelona.

00:27:36.980 --> 00:27:38.920
Most of them have
returned to Spain

00:27:38.920 --> 00:27:43.300
and become very,
very active there.

00:27:43.300 --> 00:27:46.390
One of the things we
learned is measuring e plus,

00:27:46.390 --> 00:27:50.560
e minus to mu-mu and
tau-tau, check of QED,

00:27:50.560 --> 00:27:53.080
in the time-like region.

00:27:53.080 --> 00:27:55.390
In the space-like
region, this e plus, e

00:27:55.390 --> 00:27:57.670
minus goes to e plus, e minus.

00:27:57.670 --> 00:28:00.700
And it's 1/s, and, therefore,
you take out a propagator.

00:28:00.700 --> 00:28:05.127
A 30 GeV, 20 GeV, 10 GeV shows
a perfect agreement with QED,

00:28:05.127 --> 00:28:07.210
taking into account the
[? re-data ?] corrections,

00:28:07.210 --> 00:28:08.370
of course.

00:28:08.370 --> 00:28:11.690
From this, you see the radius
of the electron and mu and tau

00:28:11.690 --> 00:28:15.702
are less than 10 to the
minus 14 centimeters.

00:28:15.702 --> 00:28:17.570
And one of the
important things was

00:28:17.570 --> 00:28:21.500
done by Greg Herten
and Harvey Newman.

00:28:21.500 --> 00:28:24.940
Harvey Newman was educated by
Lou Osborne, as you have heard.

00:28:24.940 --> 00:28:30.050
Gregor Herten and Min
Chen also worked on this.

00:28:30.050 --> 00:28:34.070
And that was first
reported by Harvey Newman

00:28:34.070 --> 00:28:38.250
in the photon conference
in Fermilab in August.

00:28:38.250 --> 00:28:40.680
And the basic idea
is very simple.

00:28:40.680 --> 00:28:44.690
You have e plus, e minus go to
anti-quark, quark, and gluon.

00:28:44.690 --> 00:28:47.920
Eventually, you see
three lobes of jets.

00:28:47.920 --> 00:28:50.570
The three loop of
jet has no meaning.

00:28:50.570 --> 00:28:51.700
You always will see that.

00:28:51.700 --> 00:28:54.360
The important thing is the
rate must agree with QCD

00:28:54.360 --> 00:28:57.790
and the shape must
agree with QCD.

00:28:57.790 --> 00:29:01.570
There was a paper published
[? rather ?] [? rapidly ?]

00:29:01.570 --> 00:29:04.410
in September, 1979.

00:29:04.410 --> 00:29:06.130
Let me define.

00:29:06.130 --> 00:29:09.920
In the production plane, since
the event has to be a planar,

00:29:09.920 --> 00:29:13.540
you have a q, gluon, q bar.

00:29:13.540 --> 00:29:17.210
And following the
terminology of Howard Georgi,

00:29:17.210 --> 00:29:21.280
you project the momentum in
this axis, which we'll would

00:29:21.280 --> 00:29:24.420
call major, along the thrust
axis, we'll call thrust.

00:29:24.420 --> 00:29:27.100
In the production plane,
the events must be circular,

00:29:27.100 --> 00:29:28.640
and your core minor.

00:29:28.640 --> 00:29:34.980
And therefore, major minus minor
is a flatness or oblateness.

00:29:34.980 --> 00:29:41.520
Oblateness essentially
transfers momentum of the gluon.

00:29:41.520 --> 00:29:43.340
And therefore, it
must be essentially

00:29:43.340 --> 00:29:47.660
0 for jet events, in which
case, major equal to minor,

00:29:47.660 --> 00:29:49.970
the event will be circular.

00:29:49.970 --> 00:29:54.370
And based on 500 events,
you see the rate indeed

00:29:54.370 --> 00:29:55.870
agrees with QCD.

00:29:55.870 --> 00:29:58.404
And without QCD,
just a quark model,

00:29:58.404 --> 00:30:00.070
no matter what you
change the parameter,

00:30:00.070 --> 00:30:01.486
you're not going
to fit this data.

00:30:06.800 --> 00:30:09.560
And this is in the
production plane.

00:30:09.560 --> 00:30:11.420
Perpendicular to the
production plane,

00:30:11.420 --> 00:30:14.806
the shape also agrees with QCD.

00:30:14.806 --> 00:30:19.040
That the shape agrees with
QCD is very important,

00:30:19.040 --> 00:30:23.410
because there are many models
essentially produce three jets.

00:30:23.410 --> 00:30:24.910
And these are the data.

00:30:24.910 --> 00:30:26.280
And this is the QCD.

00:30:26.280 --> 00:30:28.550
This is the phase
space distribution.

00:30:28.550 --> 00:30:29.925
And this is Q Q
bar distribution.

00:30:29.925 --> 00:30:34.624
Phase space, of course, also
produces three jet patterns.

00:30:34.624 --> 00:30:40.600
Indeed, with the increasing
of oblateness or the transfers

00:30:40.600 --> 00:30:43.790
of momentum of gluon,
you can see, gradually,

00:30:43.790 --> 00:30:46.700
with increasing, you begin
to see the gluon jet.

00:30:46.700 --> 00:30:49.280
When the transfers of momentum
to gluon is very small,

00:30:49.280 --> 00:30:50.830
you have no fragmentation.

00:30:50.830 --> 00:30:53.390
And gradually, you
have a small jet.

00:30:53.390 --> 00:30:56.270
Eventually, you have
a full three jet.

00:30:56.270 --> 00:31:04.510
I mention this, because you will
always see three jet events.

00:31:04.510 --> 00:31:07.850
And this is a three jet event.

00:31:07.850 --> 00:31:10.010
And this is a flat
event at 12 GeV.

00:31:10.010 --> 00:31:13.250
But this event can be completely
explained by a Q Q bar

00:31:13.250 --> 00:31:16.250
model without gluons.

00:31:16.250 --> 00:31:18.950
Indeed, before we
publish our result,

00:31:18.950 --> 00:31:20.495
there was an early
result by TASSO,

00:31:20.495 --> 00:31:24.320
by Paul Soeding,
which says they indeed

00:31:24.320 --> 00:31:28.890
find five [? flat ?] event,
where the QCD predict nine.

00:31:28.890 --> 00:31:32.120
And they said, if the
theory is correct,

00:31:32.120 --> 00:31:35.360
we must soon confirm this
result with higher statistics

00:31:35.360 --> 00:31:40.090
and with much more
improved evidence.

00:31:40.090 --> 00:31:43.660
And there's a article
by Gloria Lubkin--

00:31:43.660 --> 00:31:44.820
I saw her.

00:31:44.820 --> 00:31:47.110
I see her here--

00:31:47.110 --> 00:31:52.440
describing the support
of QCD and gluons.

00:31:52.440 --> 00:31:56.430
And this is the
data from Mark-J.

00:31:56.430 --> 00:32:01.050
And there, it said
fairly clearly.

00:32:01.050 --> 00:32:04.060
With time, people tend to
forget who discovered what.

00:32:04.060 --> 00:32:07.050
And fortunately, at
that time, Mr. Schopper

00:32:07.050 --> 00:32:08.260
was the director general.

00:32:08.260 --> 00:32:10.440
He got hold of
everybody, together,

00:32:10.440 --> 00:32:12.910
and agreed to say that--

00:32:12.910 --> 00:32:15.000
Schopper told us
that Mark-J group

00:32:15.000 --> 00:32:17.400
was the first to report
statistically significant

00:32:17.400 --> 00:32:22.530
evidence of the three jet
pattern predicted QCD.

00:32:22.530 --> 00:32:26.870
After sometime, people
tend to forget that.

00:32:26.870 --> 00:32:29.280
But today, of course,
things are very different.

00:32:29.280 --> 00:32:31.470
With higher energy,
these are the data

00:32:31.470 --> 00:32:34.040
Professor Becker
reported this morning.

00:32:34.040 --> 00:32:36.794
With high energy, the jet
are much more collimated.

00:32:36.794 --> 00:32:38.710
And you can see the jets
become much narrower.

00:32:43.460 --> 00:32:47.910
A good experiment, in fact,
also a very difficult one,

00:32:47.910 --> 00:32:51.890
was the electroweak effect
from the existence of z0.

00:32:51.890 --> 00:32:56.540
Because of the existence of z0,
the forward-backward asymmetry

00:32:56.540 --> 00:32:59.240
is a function of
[? ga. ?] At this energy,

00:32:59.240 --> 00:33:04.430
it's about minus 10%, if we
define theta as mu minus, which

00:33:04.430 --> 00:33:05.960
is back to e minus.

00:33:05.960 --> 00:33:08.110
If you want to look
for 10% effect,

00:33:08.110 --> 00:33:12.030
your systematic error
has to be better than 1%.

00:33:12.030 --> 00:33:17.160
Because of that, the
Mark-J detector actually

00:33:17.160 --> 00:33:19.800
can be rotated, both
in phi direction

00:33:19.800 --> 00:33:21.846
and also in theta
direction, to cancel

00:33:21.846 --> 00:33:22.970
all the systematic effects.

00:33:26.092 --> 00:33:28.690
The experiment e plus,
e minus to mu plus,

00:33:28.690 --> 00:33:32.010
mu minus was the thesis of
Jean-Pierre Revol, who's

00:33:32.010 --> 00:33:33.890
here, and also Gregor Herten.

00:33:42.400 --> 00:33:45.610
And this is the measurement of
e plus, e minus, two mu plus, mu

00:33:45.610 --> 00:33:46.110
minus.

00:33:46.110 --> 00:33:52.390
This was one year before the
discovery of the Z and W.

00:33:52.390 --> 00:33:54.430
First, you have to make
sure your detector has

00:33:54.430 --> 00:33:58.150
no asymmetry from cosmic rays,
and, also, the low energy data

00:33:58.150 --> 00:34:00.390
must only be explained by QED.

00:34:00.390 --> 00:34:04.030
And then high energy cannot
be explained by QED and agrees

00:34:04.030 --> 00:34:08.310
with the model of Glashow,
Weinberg, and so on.

00:34:08.310 --> 00:34:11.230
And that also was
reported in Physics Today

00:34:11.230 --> 00:34:14.316
and shows the curve which
I just presented to you.

00:34:19.750 --> 00:34:23.940
Indeed, we were able to manage
to measure the asymmetry

00:34:23.940 --> 00:34:25.469
as a function of energy.

00:34:25.469 --> 00:34:27.969
Of course, over a very
limited energy region,

00:34:27.969 --> 00:34:33.310
but, nevertheless, it agrees
with the standard model.

00:34:33.310 --> 00:34:38.940
And I think this was originally
the work of Min Chen.

00:34:38.940 --> 00:34:42.449
With this very crude data,
we were able to set a range

00:34:42.449 --> 00:34:44.650
of [? GeV ?] and [? ga ?]
Together with the neutrino

00:34:44.650 --> 00:34:49.540
data, we said the [? GeV ?]
[? ga ?] range in this region.

00:34:49.540 --> 00:34:53.040
Today, Ulrich Becker's
data shows, now, we're

00:34:53.040 --> 00:34:53.639
in this point.

00:34:59.260 --> 00:35:03.710
Much work was carried out
on the physics of gluons.

00:35:03.710 --> 00:35:07.270
One of them is a determinant of
the strong coupling constant.

00:35:07.270 --> 00:35:09.520
Strong coupling
constant, essentially,

00:35:09.520 --> 00:35:12.970
is the rate of three jet versus
two jet, because, in this case,

00:35:12.970 --> 00:35:14.800
you just measure this coupling.

00:35:14.800 --> 00:35:20.730
The first order of the strong
coupling constant is 0.2.

00:35:20.730 --> 00:35:23.200
To complete second
order, you have

00:35:23.200 --> 00:35:29.490
to take into account not only
the standard QED graph but also

00:35:29.490 --> 00:35:32.620
the three gluon vertex.

00:35:32.620 --> 00:35:38.620
When you properly take it into
account, all the second order

00:35:38.620 --> 00:35:46.990
diagrams, your data completely
agrees with QCD prediction.

00:35:46.990 --> 00:35:53.020
This was work mostly done by
Min Chen and Harvey Newman.

00:35:53.020 --> 00:35:56.170
And this is really
very difficult work.

00:35:56.170 --> 00:35:59.190
Let me just give you a feeling
what kind of systematic error

00:35:59.190 --> 00:36:01.060
that's involved.

00:36:01.060 --> 00:36:04.420
Using different fragmentation
models, different detector

00:36:04.420 --> 00:36:09.160
parameters, gluon fragmentation
function, cut-off energy

00:36:09.160 --> 00:36:11.520
transfers momentum in
the Feynman field model

00:36:11.520 --> 00:36:16.630
or in the string model, And the
change is in the 0.01 level.

00:36:16.630 --> 00:36:21.040
And this is the
difference in the change

00:36:21.040 --> 00:36:23.270
of the strong coupling
constant as a function

00:36:23.270 --> 00:36:25.930
of the cut-off in the energy
in the [? particle ?] level.

00:36:29.640 --> 00:36:32.780
And again, it was a
difficult experiment.

00:36:32.780 --> 00:36:34.370
There's always disagreement.

00:36:34.370 --> 00:36:35.390
What is plotted in here?

00:36:35.390 --> 00:36:37.556
This curve comes from
Professor Min Chen, I believe,

00:36:37.556 --> 00:36:38.530
originally.

00:36:38.530 --> 00:36:42.950
It's alpha s as
function of years.

00:36:42.950 --> 00:36:46.400
And this is the measurement
by Min Chen and Newman.

00:36:46.400 --> 00:36:49.474
And these are the
measurement by other groups.

00:36:49.474 --> 00:36:51.640
At the beginning, they were
factor of two different.

00:36:51.640 --> 00:36:53.840
Unfortunately, after
'86, now everybody

00:36:53.840 --> 00:36:57.037
agree with each other.

00:36:57.037 --> 00:36:57.870
Let's say, with him.

00:37:02.110 --> 00:37:04.280
We also look for new quarks.

00:37:04.280 --> 00:37:07.570
And new quarks can be
looked by the so-called R. R

00:37:07.570 --> 00:37:11.290
is e plus, e minus to hadron
versus e plus, e minus to mu.

00:37:11.290 --> 00:37:13.480
And they essentially
measure quark charge.

00:37:13.480 --> 00:37:15.657
With different particles,
you see sharp peaks,

00:37:15.657 --> 00:37:16.740
and you'll see a shoulder.

00:37:20.370 --> 00:37:25.020
Those days, we know
the first three

00:37:25.020 --> 00:37:27.640
quarks has a mass of
1 GeV, fourth quark

00:37:27.640 --> 00:37:29.160
has a mass of 3 GeV.

00:37:29.160 --> 00:37:31.760
The fifth quark has
a mass of 9 GeV.

00:37:31.760 --> 00:37:34.930
And there was a paper
by Glashow at that time.

00:37:34.930 --> 00:37:40.730
And it says the sixth quark,
when you take into account QCD,

00:37:40.730 --> 00:37:42.910
must be a 38 GeV.

00:37:42.910 --> 00:37:46.560
It's in the Physical
Review Letters.

00:37:46.560 --> 00:37:49.692
And this is our
measurement of R.

00:37:49.692 --> 00:37:52.870
And since we have
managed to go to 46 GeV,

00:37:52.870 --> 00:37:55.900
and you really see
nothing at all.

00:37:55.900 --> 00:38:06.070
When you take all the data
into account of the electroweak

00:38:06.070 --> 00:38:12.240
effect and QCD
corrections, and then you

00:38:12.240 --> 00:38:14.070
can set the size of the quark.

00:38:14.070 --> 00:38:16.350
And the size of the quark
will be less than 10

00:38:16.350 --> 00:38:20.320
to the minus 16 centimeters.

00:38:20.320 --> 00:38:23.720
We also look for
many new particles.

00:38:23.720 --> 00:38:28.010
And none, clearly, was found.

00:38:28.010 --> 00:38:30.990
There were many young
physicists working with us

00:38:30.990 --> 00:38:36.870
at that time, Albrecht Boehm,
HS Chen, Mr. Zhang, who is now

00:38:36.870 --> 00:38:38.740
the new director in Beijing.

00:38:38.740 --> 00:38:41.390
Peter Duinker is a professor
at University of Amsterdam.

00:38:41.390 --> 00:38:42.870
Herten is at MIT.

00:38:42.870 --> 00:38:46.069
Jean-Pierre Revol
and Wyslouch is also

00:38:46.069 --> 00:38:47.110
assistant professor here.

00:38:50.710 --> 00:38:54.580
This is a picture of the
very small Mark-J group.

00:38:57.961 --> 00:39:01.126
And there's Harvey Newman.

00:39:01.126 --> 00:39:02.310
Let me see.

00:39:02.310 --> 00:39:05.060
There's Mr. Zhang, who
is the new director.

00:39:05.060 --> 00:39:06.070
This is Peter Duinker.

00:39:06.070 --> 00:39:06.830
This is Min Chen.

00:39:06.830 --> 00:39:09.590
This is my wife, Susan.

00:39:09.590 --> 00:39:12.890
There's Ulrich Becker
and Jean-Pierre Revol.

00:39:12.890 --> 00:39:15.790
Everybody was younger then.

00:39:15.790 --> 00:39:20.780
This morning, Ulrich
Becker mentioned the effort

00:39:20.780 --> 00:39:27.890
at the lab, at the 27 GeV
e plus, e minus collider.

00:39:27.890 --> 00:39:33.020
And there, we mentioned
we have a L3 experiment.

00:39:33.020 --> 00:39:37.850
In the L3, there are
many, many physicists.

00:39:37.850 --> 00:39:41.120
Somewhat unfortunate,
so many people,

00:39:41.120 --> 00:39:43.940
not only from the United
States, from Bulgaria,

00:39:43.940 --> 00:39:47.990
from China, France, Germany,
Holland, Hungary, India, Italy,

00:39:47.990 --> 00:39:53.380
Korea, Russia, Spain,
Switzerland, and Taiwan.

00:39:53.380 --> 00:39:59.570
There are about 120 physicists
from 16 US institutions.

00:39:59.570 --> 00:40:03.130
And this is during one
of the group meetings,

00:40:03.130 --> 00:40:05.610
during the assembly
of the experiment.

00:40:05.610 --> 00:40:07.820
And this is with one
third of the physicists.

00:40:11.500 --> 00:40:18.490
And I should mention, during
this time, Robert Birgeneau,

00:40:18.490 --> 00:40:20.680
who was the head
of the department

00:40:20.680 --> 00:40:24.010
and, also, at the
beginning, Jerry Friedman,

00:40:24.010 --> 00:40:27.070
who was the director of the
Laboratory of Nuclear Science,

00:40:27.070 --> 00:40:30.580
were most supportive to get
this experiment underway.

00:40:30.580 --> 00:40:32.430
These are really very
difficult things.

00:40:32.430 --> 00:40:36.850
And particularly,
Arthur Kerman really

00:40:36.850 --> 00:40:39.490
spent a rather large
amount of effort

00:40:39.490 --> 00:40:41.370
to support this experiment.

00:40:41.370 --> 00:40:44.770
And this is a picture
of Arthur Kerman

00:40:44.770 --> 00:40:52.010
with Tom Cabot during one of
the visits to L3 experiment.

00:40:52.010 --> 00:40:55.270
And two years ago, I
think, the MIT Corporation

00:40:55.270 --> 00:40:58.330
made the executive committee
of the MIT Corporation

00:40:58.330 --> 00:40:59.980
come to visit L3.

00:40:59.980 --> 00:41:02.268
And this is during
one of the receptions.

00:41:05.076 --> 00:41:08.100
The experiment, now, had become
rather large and somewhat

00:41:08.100 --> 00:41:10.480
complicated for L3.

00:41:10.480 --> 00:41:13.100
For example, in
the last 18 months,

00:41:13.100 --> 00:41:15.240
60 papers are being
written or being

00:41:15.240 --> 00:41:17.810
published by 150 physicists.

00:41:17.810 --> 00:41:21.660
And that means, it's really no
longer possible for a person

00:41:21.660 --> 00:41:24.780
to really understand
everything in detail.

00:41:24.780 --> 00:41:27.720
Certainly not me.

00:41:27.720 --> 00:41:33.040
And Ulrich Becker mentioned
some of the major results,

00:41:33.040 --> 00:41:37.050
a number of neutrino species,
a radius of leptons and quarks,

00:41:37.050 --> 00:41:40.470
the mass of Higgs, Z parameter,
and precision determined

00:41:40.470 --> 00:41:43.530
the heavy quarks,
measurement of quark mixing,

00:41:43.530 --> 00:41:45.135
a measurement of s.

00:41:45.135 --> 00:41:52.330
And because this is a
very precise detector,

00:41:52.330 --> 00:41:54.590
you can measure
transaction metrics.

00:41:54.590 --> 00:41:57.600
There are many different ways
to measure sine square W. All

00:41:57.600 --> 00:41:58.760
agree with each other.

00:41:58.760 --> 00:42:00.675
A model independent
measured the number

00:42:00.675 --> 00:42:04.470
of neutrinos in the universe,
lepton flavor violation,

00:42:04.470 --> 00:42:11.050
flavor independent of alpha s, e
plus, e minus go to Z plus eta.

00:42:11.050 --> 00:42:13.440
This has been a very
difficult thing.

00:42:13.440 --> 00:42:15.510
Because, in the past,
nobody's [? state ?]

00:42:15.510 --> 00:42:18.104
has agreed with them on
the color or with QCD.

00:42:18.104 --> 00:42:19.770
And this is the first
time we've managed

00:42:19.770 --> 00:42:23.510
to see this is really
an experimental effect

00:42:23.510 --> 00:42:26.115
that previous results
do not agree with QCD.

00:42:26.115 --> 00:42:29.450
We obtained the branching
ratio and also studied hard

00:42:29.450 --> 00:42:31.870
photon emission, J
production, and the mass

00:42:31.870 --> 00:42:35.470
of excited electron is
larger than 122 GeV.

00:42:39.730 --> 00:42:44.430
Mr. Becker also mentioned, maybe
someone will go to LEP 200.

00:42:44.430 --> 00:42:48.830
One of the first thing we can
see, look for will be Higgs.

00:42:48.830 --> 00:42:51.130
Indeed, we have now
a very large effort

00:42:51.130 --> 00:42:53.920
to ask LEP to go as
high energy as one can.

00:42:53.920 --> 00:42:57.070
If you go 120 GeV, you
can set the mass of Higgs

00:42:57.070 --> 00:42:58.330
already to 100.

00:42:58.330 --> 00:43:02.330
If the machine's 220 GeV,
you can set the mass of Higgs

00:43:02.330 --> 00:43:04.861
to 120 GeV.

00:43:04.861 --> 00:43:10.490
The second thing, it's a very
precise test of the electroweak

00:43:10.490 --> 00:43:16.081
theory, the comparisons
for W from 100 and LEP 200.

00:43:16.081 --> 00:43:19.840
LEP 100, with a field 10 to
the 6th or 10 to the 7th event,

00:43:19.840 --> 00:43:22.680
you can measure the
mass of the Z to 15 MeV.

00:43:22.680 --> 00:43:25.290
Measure the parameters,
various parameters

00:43:25.290 --> 00:43:30.270
enable you to determine sines
for W to 2 plus/minus 0.005.

00:43:30.270 --> 00:43:34.810
And LEP 200 measures e plus,
e minus to W pair by measuring

00:43:34.810 --> 00:43:38.070
the mass of W to 50 MeV.

00:43:38.070 --> 00:43:42.110
Since sine squared W is 1
minus mw divided by the mz,

00:43:42.110 --> 00:43:45.250
you determine sine
square W to 0.003.

00:43:45.250 --> 00:43:47.616
This number must agree
with this number.

00:43:50.472 --> 00:43:54.210
The third thing if gauge
cancellation, which

00:43:54.210 --> 00:43:55.920
Ulrich Becker also mentioned.

00:43:55.920 --> 00:44:02.390
And that is the three diagrams
must cancel each other,

00:44:02.390 --> 00:44:04.780
so the cross-section
is constant.

00:44:04.780 --> 00:44:06.720
And that is most
sensitively measured

00:44:06.720 --> 00:44:09.365
by measuring the
forward-backward asymmetry.

00:44:09.365 --> 00:44:13.600
And then particularly, if
there's no triple boson

00:44:13.600 --> 00:44:15.880
coupling, the rate in
the background region

00:44:15.880 --> 00:44:16.920
will vastly increase.

00:44:21.980 --> 00:44:29.330
So since we come to MIT,
the first thing we have done

00:44:29.330 --> 00:44:32.460
is to measure photon to
the electron pair test QED

00:44:32.460 --> 00:44:36.030
to the [INAUDIBLE],, define
the radius 10 to minus 14,

00:44:36.030 --> 00:44:39.710
measure rho, omega, phi to
e plus, e minus, check SU3.

00:44:39.710 --> 00:44:43.470
Build a spectrometer that has a
mass resolution of 1%, hydrogen

00:44:43.470 --> 00:44:46.150
rejection 1 part in 10 to the
8th, luminosity is about 10

00:44:46.150 --> 00:44:47.180
to the 35.

00:44:47.180 --> 00:44:51.450
Measurement was always done
twice, once with momentum,

00:44:51.450 --> 00:44:53.875
another time with shower.

00:44:53.875 --> 00:44:57.570
At Brookhaven, the detector
has a mass resolution

00:44:57.570 --> 00:44:58.365
of 1 part in 1,000.

00:44:58.365 --> 00:45:00.720
The rejection is 1
part in 10 to the 10th.

00:45:00.720 --> 00:45:02.940
The luminosity is 10 to the 36.

00:45:02.940 --> 00:45:05.140
Again, we develop
a high rate chamber

00:45:05.140 --> 00:45:09.720
but duplicate the measurement
to obtain the high rate.

00:45:09.720 --> 00:45:12.540
ISR is the first time
we enter into 2 pi

00:45:12.540 --> 00:45:15.190
detector with a
very good rejection

00:45:15.190 --> 00:45:18.760
and developed large
area chambers.

00:45:18.760 --> 00:45:22.550
At PETRA, that's the time
we developed the 4 pi

00:45:22.550 --> 00:45:25.310
detector for discovery
of gluon and e plus, e

00:45:25.310 --> 00:45:27.065
minus to mu plus, mu minus.

00:45:27.065 --> 00:45:30.640
We set the radius
10 to the minus 16.

00:45:30.640 --> 00:45:34.820
Now we check the
electroweak theory to 1%,

00:45:34.820 --> 00:45:37.360
through the P P bar mixings,
set the mass of Higgs.

00:45:37.360 --> 00:45:40.530
We have a 4 pi detector that
measures e, mu, gamma to 1%.

00:45:43.240 --> 00:45:49.110
For the future, as Gregor
Herten had mentioned,

00:45:49.110 --> 00:45:54.300
we will stay till '94
of '95 at LEP 100,

00:45:54.300 --> 00:45:56.540
field 10 to the 6th
or 10 to the 7th.

00:45:56.540 --> 00:46:00.720
For three to four years, maybe
five, to W pair, at which time,

00:46:00.720 --> 00:46:03.160
there are basically
quite a few choices.

00:46:03.160 --> 00:46:07.900
One choice, no LHC, in
which case, the machine

00:46:07.900 --> 00:46:12.740
will go to 36 [INAUDIBLE] and
36 [INAUDIBLE],, both at W and Z,

00:46:12.740 --> 00:46:15.850
and with a luminosity
larger than 10 to the 36.

00:46:15.850 --> 00:46:18.500
Another possibility,
in fact, indeed,

00:46:18.500 --> 00:46:22.350
may rightly [? happen ?] you for
new discovery coming [? HERA ?]

00:46:22.350 --> 00:46:23.890
and to do EP.

00:46:23.890 --> 00:46:26.530
Another is the phase
one, phase two of LEP,

00:46:26.530 --> 00:46:28.660
with Gregor Herten mentioned.

00:46:28.660 --> 00:46:30.550
Let me just make
a comment of EP.

00:46:36.280 --> 00:46:37.970
In this century
since Rutherford,

00:46:37.970 --> 00:46:40.250
since Hofstadter, and
Kendall, Friedman, Taylor,

00:46:40.250 --> 00:46:43.625
much work, much discovery
has been made from EP.

00:46:43.625 --> 00:46:49.116
At LHC, with 0.06 TeV
electron, with 8 TeV proton,

00:46:49.116 --> 00:46:52.950
with a luminosity 10 to
the 32, since the electron

00:46:52.950 --> 00:46:57.605
is stationary, and, therefore,
the electron goes everywhere,

00:46:57.605 --> 00:46:59.870
and with the low
luminosity, basically, you

00:46:59.870 --> 00:47:03.980
do not need to make
any modification of L3,

00:47:03.980 --> 00:47:06.370
but, with the adding of a
forward detector, measure

00:47:06.370 --> 00:47:07.430
protons.

00:47:07.430 --> 00:47:12.550
Compared to HERA, the center
mass energy is factor 20 high.

00:47:12.550 --> 00:47:17.180
And we have also looked
into what the detector will

00:47:17.180 --> 00:47:20.750
look like for EP.

00:47:20.750 --> 00:47:22.720
Essentially, the
BGO, vertex chamber,

00:47:22.720 --> 00:47:24.930
has calorimeter muon chamber.

00:47:24.930 --> 00:47:27.680
It can be kept the same,
except, in the forward part,

00:47:27.680 --> 00:47:30.907
for the proton, we add
a proton calorimeter.

00:47:35.110 --> 00:47:38.700
Gregor mentioned,
if you go to LHC,

00:47:38.700 --> 00:47:42.970
there's a very simple design
by adding six Tesla coil

00:47:42.970 --> 00:47:47.540
and putting colorimeter
and vertex chamber.

00:47:47.540 --> 00:47:52.030
In this way, because you
have six Tesla coil, what

00:47:52.030 --> 00:47:57.010
will happen is you will trap
all the mu, low energy particle,

00:47:57.010 --> 00:47:58.990
and let them go
forward and backward.

00:47:58.990 --> 00:48:03.590
Only muons go out
into your detector.

00:48:03.590 --> 00:48:08.930
Another design is to measure
the electrons and photons very

00:48:08.930 --> 00:48:12.794
precisely by putting
crystals very, very far away

00:48:12.794 --> 00:48:13.460
from the vertex.

00:48:15.980 --> 00:48:18.980
The basic idea is very simple.

00:48:18.980 --> 00:48:23.340
At a distance of 3 meters,
a 25 GeV pi 0 go to 2 gamma.

00:48:23.340 --> 00:48:25.700
The 2 gamma will be in
2 different crystals,

00:48:25.700 --> 00:48:27.493
and, therefore, you
can reject the pi 0.

00:48:31.357 --> 00:48:35.960
Well, I should mention, since
there are many detectors now,

00:48:35.960 --> 00:48:43.600
we will proceed for L3
detector upgrade at PP or eP,

00:48:43.600 --> 00:48:49.330
only if we have a unique
design, with unique resolution,

00:48:49.330 --> 00:48:53.560
unique design concept, not
to the other general purpose

00:48:53.560 --> 00:48:56.260
detectors, and,
also, a minimum cost.

00:48:56.260 --> 00:48:59.750
If we cannot achieve that, we
probably will not proceed with

00:48:59.750 --> 00:49:00.250
this.

00:49:00.250 --> 00:49:03.640
And we will do something else.

00:49:03.640 --> 00:49:07.960
I must mention that much of the
result from our collaboration

00:49:07.960 --> 00:49:12.520
comes from doing instrumentation
development, which

00:49:12.520 --> 00:49:15.410
is something Viki always
think is very, very important.

00:49:15.410 --> 00:49:17.410
I totally agree with him.

00:49:17.410 --> 00:49:20.310
And all done by Ulrich
Becker and collaborators.

00:49:20.310 --> 00:49:24.790
Since '74, developed the high
rate chambers, '76, large drift

00:49:24.790 --> 00:49:29.000
chamber, '79, drift tubes,
now know as straw tubes,

00:49:29.000 --> 00:49:30.400
originally developed by him.

00:49:30.400 --> 00:49:34.300
In '82, he developed the L3
multisampling drift chambers.

00:49:34.300 --> 00:49:44.240
And now he has a
continuing effort for R&amp;D.

00:49:44.240 --> 00:49:50.510
Now, finally, let me make
a few small comments.

00:49:50.510 --> 00:49:54.540
From my personal experience,
when I first come to MIT,

00:49:54.540 --> 00:49:57.000
the group only had four people.

00:49:57.000 --> 00:49:58.740
Now there are 600.

00:49:58.740 --> 00:50:02.730
In fact, when I first come
to MIT, none of the research

00:50:02.730 --> 00:50:08.210
really was funded
by DOE, at all.

00:50:08.210 --> 00:50:11.700
Under the funding, we were
talking about 10 to the 4.

00:50:11.700 --> 00:50:12.790
Now, it's 10 to the 8.

00:50:12.790 --> 00:50:15.870
And the detectors come
from a few coincidence

00:50:15.870 --> 00:50:19.240
to half a million channels.

00:50:19.240 --> 00:50:27.640
Indeed, if you look, a number of
physicists collaborate with me.

00:50:27.640 --> 00:50:31.750
And since I come to MIT,
really, unfortunately, it

00:50:31.750 --> 00:50:36.038
has gone up so enormously.

00:50:36.038 --> 00:50:38.550
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:50:38.550 --> 00:50:41.070
TING: Not the [? right ?]
population, no.

00:50:41.070 --> 00:50:47.100
Clearly, this cannot have
been possible without a strong

00:50:47.100 --> 00:50:50.910
support of the Department of
Energy and Bill Wallenmeyer,

00:50:50.910 --> 00:50:55.260
who is here, Bernie Hildebrand,
Bill Hess, John O'Fallon, PK,

00:50:55.260 --> 00:50:57.336
and Enloe Ritter.

00:50:57.336 --> 00:51:06.020
I have a nice picture of Bill
Wallenmeyer, PK Williams.

00:51:06.020 --> 00:51:08.650
Had a conversation with
my good friend Okun.

00:51:08.650 --> 00:51:11.160
He was doing one of
the visits of L3.

00:51:11.160 --> 00:51:14.290
But unfortunately for
us, after this visit,

00:51:14.290 --> 00:51:17.060
he decided to take $1
million out of our budget.

00:51:19.670 --> 00:51:24.640
It was probably because we
served the wrong coffee.

00:51:24.640 --> 00:51:27.710
And I also wanted to
take this opportunity

00:51:27.710 --> 00:51:34.820
to thank the MIT administration
and also the Dean of Sciences,

00:51:34.820 --> 00:51:43.060
as well as the MIT Physics
Department, Vicki, Herman,

00:51:43.060 --> 00:51:45.890
Francis, Jerry Friedman,
R. Birgeneau, Ernie

00:51:45.890 --> 00:51:51.040
Moniz, and also
Peter Demos, Arthur,

00:51:51.040 --> 00:51:53.260
Fred Eppling, and the
staff of Laboratory Nuclear

00:51:53.260 --> 00:51:57.130
Science, particularly most
of the nonscientific staff.

00:51:57.130 --> 00:52:00.850
Because for them,
it's not for us.

00:52:00.850 --> 00:52:03.130
We do things for fun.

00:52:03.130 --> 00:52:08.440
And L3 has become very
internationally visible.

00:52:08.440 --> 00:52:11.350
And this is a visit to
L3 by the US Congress

00:52:11.350 --> 00:52:13.390
Committee on Science
and Technology,

00:52:13.390 --> 00:52:18.680
just the Foreign International
Collaboration Subcommittee.

00:52:18.680 --> 00:52:21.980
Anyway, they come
with their own jet.

00:52:21.980 --> 00:52:25.570
Finally, let me
mention, in 28 years,

00:52:25.570 --> 00:52:29.235
the most rewarding thing
I had was to win $40.

00:52:33.480 --> 00:52:37.810
My first $10 was October '65.

00:52:37.810 --> 00:52:40.310
I bet with Leon
Lederman on how long

00:52:40.310 --> 00:52:43.350
it would take to complete
the QED experiment.

00:52:43.350 --> 00:52:46.420
And the second was
August '74, bet

00:52:46.420 --> 00:52:49.690
with $10 with Mel
Schwartz on J particle.

00:52:49.690 --> 00:52:52.397
And the third was $20 from Viki.

00:52:52.397 --> 00:52:53.230
Thank you very much.

00:52:53.230 --> 00:52:55.080
[APPLAUSE]

